From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: emuTOS-MCD <darekadumon@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 12 Aug 2026 21:36:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 087/190] segacd: mask the payload transfer -- the last
 unguarded CDC sequence

'Dfree -11, Fsfirst -33, Fopen -33' is two different faults, and the
-33 is the one nothing here could see: a directory that reads without
error and does not contain a file sitting in its first entry means the
bytes were wrong, not the read.

_segacd_cdc_read streams 1026 words out of the host register with
interrupts enabled. Level 4 arrives every 13 ms and level 5 on every
decoded sector; both read the CDC register file, level 5 alone doing
eleven reads of 0xFF8007 inside that window. The header words leave
first and arrive intact, so the sector passes every check this driver
has while its payload is disturbed behind them -- silent corruption
with a valid header, which is exactly the -33 signature.

Every foreground register sequence in segacd.c was masked days ago
against precisely this hazard. The payload transfer, the longest and
most exposed of them all, was left open. Sega's CDCTRN cannot hit it
because it runs FROM the decoder interrupt; ours runs underneath it.

About 1.5 ms masked at 12.5 MHz -- one exchange window, and the drive
tolerates a late read. It is a late COMMAND that kills the link, and
none are sent from here.
---
 bios/segacd2.S | 21 +++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 21 insertions(+)

diff --git a/bios/segacd2.S b/bios/segacd2.S
index 98b0a7c..b682bbc 100644
--- a/bios/segacd2.S
+++ b/bios/segacd2.S
@@ -98,11 +98,31 @@ _segacd_int_5:
 //   hdr   <- two words, the sector's M/S/F/mode header
 //   data  <- (groups+1) * 8 words
         .globl  _segacd_cdc_read
+// Masked for its whole length, which it was not until now.
+//
+// The loop below streams 1026 words out of the CDC's host register with
+// interrupts enabled. Level 4 arrives every 13 ms and level 5 on every
+// decoded sector, and BOTH read the CDC register file -- level 5 does
+// eleven reads of 0xFF8007 by itself. The header words leave first and
+// arrive intact, so nothing downstream can tell: the sector passes the
+// header check with a disturbed payload behind it, which is exactly
+// what "Fsfirst -33, file not in a directory that read fine" is.
+//
+// Every foreground register sequence in segacd.c was masked days ago
+// for precisely this hazard; the payload transfer, the longest and
+// most exposed of them, was left open. Sega's CDCTRN cannot hit this
+// because it runs FROM the decoder interrupt. Ours runs underneath it.
+//
+// About 1.5 ms at 12.5 MHz -- one exchange window at most, and the
+// drive tolerates a late read; it is a late COMMAND that kills the
+// link, and none are sent from here.
 _segacd_cdc_read:
         move.l  4(sp),a1                // header destination
         move.l  8(sp),a0                // data destination
         move.w  14(sp),d1               // low word of the long count
         move.l  a2,-(sp)
+        move.w  sr,-(sp)
+        move.w  #0x2700,sr
         lea     0xFF8008,a2
         move.w  (a2),(a1)+
         nop
@@ -124,6 +144,7 @@ _segacd_cdc_read:
         move.w  (a2),(a0)+
         nop
         dbra    d1,1b
+        move.w  (sp)+,sr
         move.l  (sp)+,a2
         rts
 
